---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import HubPageShell from '../components/hub/HubPageShell.astro';

const nav = [
  { id: 'why-varies', label: 'Why behavior varies' },
  { id: 'movement', label: 'Movement vs failure' },
  { id: 'phx', label: 'Central Arizona' },
  { id: 'tuc', label: 'Southern Arizona' },
  { id: 'naz', label: 'Northern Arizona' },
  { id: 'king', label: 'Northwestern Arizona' },
  { id: 'white', label: 'White Mountains' },
  { id: 'yuma', label: 'Southwestern Arizona' },
  { id: 'four', label: 'Four Corners' },
  { id: 'scott', label: 'Foothill communities' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Foundation Behavior in Arizona"
  description="How soil conditions influence foundation movement across Arizona."
  hub={true}
  quote="In Arizona, the story of a foundation begins with the soil and the moisture that moves through it."
>
  <HubPageShell
    title="Foundation Behavior in Arizona"
    subhead="Soil, climate, and movement across the state"
    description="Across much of Arizona, foundation problems are driven by soil behavior and moisture variability rather than structural collapse."
    glance={[
      { label: 'Central', value: 'Expansive clay basins' },
      { label: 'South', value: 'Caliche and collapsible soils' },
      { label: 'Pressure', value: 'Sign-today estimates' },
      { label: 'Next', value: 'Know Before You Sign' },
    ]}
    nav={nav}
    topicsStyle="list"
  >
    <div class="accordion-section" id="why-varies">
      <div class="accordion-header">
        <span class="accordion-title">Why foundation behavior varies across Arizona</span>
        <span class="accordion-icon">▸</span>
      </div>
      <div class="accordion-content">
        <div class="accordion-content-inner content-block">
          <p>Three primary variables shape foundation performance across the state:</p>
          <p><strong>Soil composition.</strong> Expansive clay basins, caliche-rich soils, sandy and silty desert materials, decomposed granite in foothill communities, volcanic soils at higher elevations, and sedimentary plateau formations in the northeast. Each responds differently to moisture.</p>
          <p><strong>Moisture patterns.</strong> Extended dry periods, seasonal monsoons, irrigation, snowmelt in northern regions, and drainage conditions all influence soil expansion and contraction.</p>
          <p><strong>Development and site preparation.</strong> Mass grading, cut-and-fill pads, hillside construction, and varying foundation types introduce additional variability from one property to another.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="accordion-section" id="movement">
      <div class="accordion-header">
        <span class="accordion-title">Movement vs “foundation failure”</span>
        <span class="accordion-icon">▸</span>
      </div>
      <div class="accordion-content">
        <div class="accordion-content-inner content-block">
          <p>Across Arizona, some degree of soil-related movement is common. Visible cracking, minor settlement, or seasonal shifts do not automatically indicate structural instability or foundation failure.</p>
          <p>True failure involves loss of load-carrying capacity, structural instability, or progressive, non-recoverable distress. Most observed movement reflects soil response to moisture variability.</p>
          <div class="related-links">
            <a href="/how-the-industry-has-changed">How the industry has changed →</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="accordion-section" id="phx">
      <div class="accordion-header">
        <span class="accordion-title">Central Arizona — Phoenix metropolitan area</span>
        <span class="accordion-icon">▸</span>
      </div>
      <div class="accordion-content">
        <div class="accordion-content-inner content-block">
          <p>Phoenix, Mesa, Chandler, Gilbert, Tempe, Glendale, Peoria, Surprise, Goodyear, Avondale, Litchfield Park.</p>
          <p>Central Arizona is characterized by expansive clay basins and highly variable near-surface soils: expansive clays, layered alluvium, and cut-and-fill materials in master-planned developments.</p>
          <p>Seasonal soil movement is common. Cracking often reflects moisture imbalance rather than structural instability. Irrigation patterns influence the seasonal zone of wetting.</p>
          <p>Repair discussions here often include pier systems, underpinning, grade-beam reinforcement, perimeter stabilization, and moisture management. Movement in this region is frequently cyclical and moisture-driven rather than progressive.</p>
          <div class="related-links">
            <a href="/all-articles/foundation-soil-behavior-in-the-phoenix-metropolitan-area">Phoenix metropolitan soils →</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="accordion-section" id="tuc">
      <div class="accordion-header">
        <span class="accordion-title">Southern Arizona — Tucson and surrounding areas</span>
        <span class="accordion-icon">▸</span>
      </div>
      <div class="accordion-content">
        <div class="accordion-content-inner content-block">
          <p>Tucson, Oro Valley, Sierra Vista, Green Valley, Benson.</p>
          <p>Southern Arizona soils commonly include caliche layers, sandy and silty desert materials, and collapsible soils in localized areas.</p>
          <p>Movement may follow intense rainfall. Drainage conditions strongly influence soil response. Settlement patterns often differ from central Arizona’s expansive clay behavior.</p>
          <div class="related-links">
            <a href="/all-articles/southern-arizona-soil-conditions-and-structural-behavior">Southern Arizona soils →</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="accordion-section" id="naz">
      <div class="accordion-header">
        <span class="accordion-title">Northern Arizona</span>
        <span class="accordion-icon">▸</span>
      </div>
      <div class="accordion-content">
        <div class="accordion-content-inner content-block">
          <p>Flagstaff, Prescott, Payson, Show Low, Munds Park.</p>
          <p>Northern Arizona is influenced by elevation and climate: volcanic soils, mountain and forest soils, frost-affected materials. Freeze–thaw cycles and seasonal moisture variation affect soil volume. Movement may be temperature-driven rather than irrigation-driven.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="accordion-section" id="king">
      <div class="accordion-header">
        <span class="accordion-title">Northwestern Arizona</span>
        <span class="accordion-icon">▸</span>
      </div>
      <div class="accordion-content">
        <div class="accordion-content-inner content-block">
          <p>Kingman, Lake Havasu City.</p>
          <p>Granular desert soils, mixed sedimentary formations, and variable compaction in developed areas. Movement may be influenced by grading variability. Expansive clay is less regionally dominant than in central Arizona.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="accordion-section" id="white">
      <div class="accordion-header">
        <span class="accordion-title">Eastern Arizona — White Mountains</span>
        <span class="accordion-icon">▸</span>
      </div>
      <div class="accordion-content">
        <div class="accordion-content-inner content-block">
          <p>Show Low, Pinetop-Lakeside, Springerville, Eagar, Heber-Overgaard.</p>
          <p>High-elevation terrain with volcanic and cinder-based soils, forest soils, frost-affected materials, and variable depth to rock. Snowmelt and freeze–thaw matter more here than basin clay.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="accordion-section" id="yuma">
      <div class="accordion-header">
        <span class="accordion-title">Southwestern Arizona</span>
        <span class="accordion-icon">▸</span>
      </div>
      <div class="accordion-content">
        <div class="accordion-content-inner content-block">
          <p>Yuma, Somerton, San Luis.</p>
          <p>Desert alluvium, irrigation influence from agricultural activity, granular and mixed soil profiles. Settlement may vary with fill thickness and drainage. Movement can differ significantly between adjacent properties.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="accordion-section" id="four">
      <div class="accordion-header">
        <span class="accordion-title">Northeastern Arizona — Four Corners</span>
        <span class="accordion-icon">▸</span>
      </div>
      <div class="accordion-content">
        <div class="accordion-content-inner content-block">
          <p>Page, Kayenta, Chinle, Tuba City.</p>
          <p>Plateau and sedimentary environments: shallow bedrock, layered sedimentary soils, variable clay content. Movement is often highly site-specific and differs from basin-wide expansive clay behavior.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="accordion-section" id="scott">
      <div class="accordion-header">
        <span class="accordion-title">Scottsdale, Cave Creek, Fountain Hills, and foothill communities</span>
        <span class="accordion-icon">▸</span>
      </div>
      <div class="accordion-content">
        <div class="accordion-content-inner content-block">
          <p>Decomposed granite soils, slope-influenced drainage, and cut-and-fill transitions along a single structure. Soil layering can vary dramatically over short distances. Movement here is often terrain-driven rather than basin-clay-driven.</p>
          <div class="related-links">
            <a href="/know-before-you-sign">Know before you sign →</a>
            <a href="/investigate-further-arizona">Investigate further in Arizona →</a>
            <a href="/start-here">Start Here →</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </HubPageShell>
</BaseLayout>
